Never take the radio down on a healthy boot

Stock sends exactly one SET_RADIO_POWER per stack for the life of the capture:

    android   2.8s  ch0xf5  02 00 00 00 00 00
    android   2.8s  ch0xf6  02 00 00 00 00 00

and nothing else, ever. We sent two per stack:

    pmOS      2.4s  ch0xf5  01 00 00 00 00 00      <-- OFF, stock never sends this
    pmOS      3.4s  ch0xf5  02 00 00 00 00 00

MM_SIT_RADIO_STATE_OFF is 1 and ON is 2, so the first frame is an explicit
radio-down that stock has no equivalent for. It came from mm_sit_cp_prepare():
boot_ready() dropped stack 0's radio to discard contexts inherited from a
previous MM process, and slots_ready() dropped stack 1's before the DSDS
inventory -- unconditionally, because that branch was tested before the
"both stacks already mapped" success case.

The radios only actually have to be down to *write* a logical slot mapping, so
that is the only branch that takes them down now. A boot whose stacks are
already mapped -- every healthy boot -- sends one GET_SIM_SLOT_STATUS after the
boot config and completes, leaving a single `02` per stack on the wire, which is
byte-for-byte what stock sends.

The mapping path keeps both radios down, stack 1 then stack 0, tracked by
`secondary_off` and the new `primary_off`, and is still reached only after five
inventories have disagreed. Dropping a context on an MM restart is no longer
done at all; stock does not do it either.

The fake CP peer asserted the old order request by request, so it is rewritten
to the new one. That strictness is what caught this: the first build after the
change failed on `(589 == 2048)` -- GET_SIM_SLOT_STATUS arriving where
SET_RADIO_POWER was expected.

